8-bit PWM Generator with Verilator
==================================

A lightweight hardware simulation project implementing an 8-bit Pulse-Width Modulation (PWM) counter in Verilog, driven by a C++ testbench using Verilator.

Project Structure
=================

pwm.v — The Verilog hardware module (8-bit free-running counter + comparator).
sim_main.cpp — The C++ testbench that drives the clock, updates the duty cycle, and records waveforms.
Makefile — Optional automation for building and running the simulation.

Prerequisites
=============

Ensure you have the following installed on your system (Arch Linux / Omarchy):
verilator
make
A C++ compiler (g++ or clang++)

gtkwave (optional, for viewing waveforms)

-----Quick Start-----

1. Manual Compilation & Execution
=================================

Run the following Verilator command to parse the Verilog code, generate the C++ harness, compile it, and build the executable:
verilator --cc --exe --build -j 0 -Wall sim_main.cpp pwm.v


2. Run the Simulation
====================

Execute the compiled binary inside the build folder:
./obj_dir/Vpwm
This will run the simulation for a set number of clock cycles, dynamically adjust the duty cycle, and output a waveform.vcd file.

3. View Waveforms
=================

You can inspect the generated VCD trace using GTKWave:
gtkwave waveform.vcd


How It Works
============

Hardware (pwm.v): Increments an 8-bit register on every positive clock edge. Outputs a high signal (pwm_out) whenever the counter value is less than the duty threshold.

Software (sim_main.cpp): Toggles the clock signal in a loop, steps the Verilator evaluation context, modifies the duty input halfway through to show dynamic control, and dumps state changes into a VCD trace stream.
